# This repo has a public remote, and the pipeline it belongs to handles site
# forms whose locational data is legally restricted. So the policy is
# deny-by-default for anything data-shaped: code, config, and docs are tracked,
# research material never is. Committing data should take a deliberate act
# rather than remembering to add a rule.
#
# Need to commit something matched below? `git add -f <path>` — after checking
# that it really carries no site data.
#
# Note: .gitignore only governs *untracked* files. If something sensitive is
# already tracked, `git rm --cached <path>` first, and rewrite history if it has
# been pushed.

# --- Environment, caches, build artifacts ----------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- This tool's own output ------------------------------------------------
# Segment maps name every site in a batch and the pages its record occupies.
runs/
output/
out/
*.segments.json

# --- Documents and scans ---------------------------------------------------
# Includes the page images rendered for vision mode, if any are ever written
# to disk rather than held in memory.
*.pdf
*.doc
*.docx
*.rtf
*.odt
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.bmp
*.tif
*.tiff

# --- Tabular and statistical data ------------------------------------------
*.csv
*.tsv
*.xls
*.xlsx
*.xlsm
*.ods
*.parquet
*.feather
*.dta
*.sav
*.rds
*.RData
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# --- Structured pipeline data ----------------------------------------------
# Every JSON here is data, not config: segment maps, run metadata, inventory
# rows. Nothing this program needs in order to run is a .json file.
*.json
*.jsonl
*.ndjson

# --- Extracted text and logs -----------------------------------------------
# pdf_ocr writes page text as .txt; logs quote prompts and document contents.
# The prompt templates in segment_types/ are also .txt — they are code, not
# corpus data, and are re-allowed at the bottom of this file.
*.txt
*.log

# --- Geospatial ------------------------------------------------------------
# Site coordinates are the most restricted material in the corpus.
*.shp
*.shx
*.dbf
*.prj
*.cpg
*.sbn
*.sbx
*.qpj
*.kml
*.kmz
*.gpkg
*.geojson
*.gml
*.gdb/

# --- Archives (an export, zipped, is still an export) ----------------------
*.zip
*.7z
*.rar
*.tar
*.gz
*.tgz
*.bz2

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
credentials*
secrets*

# --- Editor, R, and OS cruft -----------------------------------------------
.Rhistory
.Rproj.user/
.Rproj
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/
*.swp
*~
~$*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Always keep: what this program needs to run ---------------------------
# Explicit, so no broad rule above can ever quietly drop one of them. The
# prompt templates matter most here: they are .txt, a new one would otherwise
# be silently untracked, and a segmentation pass is not reproducible without
# the exact prompt that produced it.
!.gitignore
!README.md
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!segment_types/
!segment_types/*.txt
